llama.cpp (as fetched by Ollama v0.35.1, llama.cpp b11232, applied after
112): Gemma 4's dense MLP runs on the GPU while the CPU computes the layer's
experts, as 112 arranges for Qwen's shared expert. Not gfx803-specific code;
measured on gfx803.

WHY
---
Gemma 4 26B-A4B runs a dense MLP beside the MoE in every layer, both fed
from the attention output. The graph adds the two at the end of the layer,
but build_moe_ffn expands the router and the routed experts into the graph
before that, so the dense MLP's nodes come after the experts. With the
experts of most layers in system RAM, the scheduler therefore runs the dense
MLP in the GPU split after the CPU split: the GPU idles while the CPU
computes the experts, then runs the MLP while the CPU waits. On an RX 590
the dense MLP is about 130 us of GPU work per layer (its gate, up and down
projections and norms), and at the placement llama.cpp's fit picks for a 32k
context 22 of the 30 layers have their experts in system RAM.

WHAT
----
The gemma4 graph expands the experts' input norm before the router, and
hands the dense MLP to build_moe_ffn as moe_expand_after_weights (patch
112), which expands it right after the router weights. The MLP then belongs
to the tail of the GPU split before the CPU split, which 112's early host
inputs run while the CPU computes the experts. The CPU split reads the
experts' input norm, and 112 ends the head of the GPU split at the last node
the CPU split reads, so the norm goes before the router: expanded after the
MLP it would pull the MLP into the head.

Nothing is computed differently: the same kernels run on the same inputs,
in a different order on the stream.

VERIFIED ON REAL HARDWARE (RX 590), Gemma 4 26B-A4B-it heretic-ara
i1-Q4_K_M, llama-server with fit at a 32k context, q8_0 KV cache, in rocm-gfx803-ollama:cand145 with libllama
built with and without this patch:
- Eight ~2,000-token fiction prompts, 300 greedy tokens, two rounds
  alternating: generation 20.66 / 20.61 -> 22.03 / 22.06 tok/s, the same
  text for all eight prompts, prompts 305 / 304 tok/s.
- rocprof, 299 generated tokens each: the dense MLP's kernels now run
  between the MoE top-k and the CPU wait; the 24 waits of 0.3 ms or more per
  token add up to 21.9 ms instead of 26.2 ms, a token takes 45.1 instead of
  48.9 ms, and the GPU's own work is unchanged (16.4 ms).
- llama-perplexity KL divergence against the CPU backend: 0.299922 with 2
  chunks of 2048 tokens (prompt batches of 512) and 0.416284 with 8 chunks
  of 512 tokens at -ub 1, the same digits as without this patch.
- In the image built with this patch, against the image before it,
  alternating: Gemma 4 generation 20.41 / 20.59 -> 22.06 / 22.00 tok/s with
  the same text for all eight prompts; the KL divergence figures above with
  the image's libllama; Qwen3.6 35B-A3B (graph not changed) the same text
  for all eight prompts, 39.63 / 39.50 tok/s. No GPU fault.
diff --git a/src/models/gemma4.cpp b/src/models/gemma4.cpp
index 67de74c..e0b5cff 100644
--- a/src/models/gemma4.cpp
+++ b/src/models/gemma4.cpp
@@ -312,6 +312,10 @@ llama_model_gemma4::graph::graph(const llama_model & model, const llm_graph_para
                     model.layers[il].ffn_pre_norm_2, nullptr,
                     LLM_NORM_RMS, il);
             cb(cur_moe, "ffn_norm_2", il);
+            // The experts' input before the router and the dense MLP right after it: with experts in system
+            // RAM the dense MLP then runs on the GPU while the CPU computes them, instead of after them.
+            ggml_build_forward_expand(gf, cur_moe);
+            moe_expand_after_weights = cur_mlp;
 
             // custom MoE logits calculation (router operates on attn_out, not cur)
             ggml_tensor * tmp = ggml_rms_norm(ctx0, attn_out, hparams.f_norm_rms_eps);
@@ -335,6 +339,7 @@ llama_model_gemma4::graph::graph(const llama_model & model, const llm_graph_para
                     model.layers[il].ffn_up_exps_s,
                     model.layers[il].ffn_gate_exps_s,
                     model.layers[il].ffn_down_exps_s);
+            moe_expand_after_weights = nullptr;
             cur_moe = build_norm(cur_moe,
                     model.layers[il].ffn_post_norm_2, nullptr,
                     LLM_NORM_RMS, il);
